1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,334
On eclipse day, clouds covered Iceland.

2
00:00:02,368 --> 00:00:04,342
People on the ground saw almost nothing.

3
00:00:04,365 --> 00:00:08,348
But nearly ten miles above them,
a NASA jet was racing the Moon's shadow.

4
00:00:08,475 --> 00:00:14,106
On August 12, 2026, a total solar eclipse
crossed Greenland, Iceland and Spain.

5
00:00:14,234 --> 00:00:17,531
The Moon's shadow started near
the North Pole and swept south

6
00:00:17,589 --> 00:00:20,643
over the North Atlantic,
an ocean where clouds are common.

7
00:00:20,759 --> 00:00:22,860
NASA wanted to see the Sun's corona.

8
00:00:22,883 --> 00:00:26,540
That's the faint outer atmosphere you
can only see when the Moon blocks

9
00:00:26,587 --> 00:00:27,608
the bright surface.

10
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:31,846
It's heated to nearly a million degrees,
far hotter than the surface below it,

11
00:00:31,881 --> 00:00:34,029
and scientists still don't fully know why.

12
00:00:34,064 --> 00:00:38,870
So pilots from NASA's Johnson Space
Center flew a WB-57 research jet

13
00:00:38,917 --> 00:00:40,264
from Houston to Iceland.

14
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:44,165
The WB-57 is a high-altitude
plane with a long, thin wing.

15
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:48,809
In its nose sat four cameras, built to take
at least twenty images every second.

16
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:52,605
On the ground, totality lasted two
minutes and eighteen seconds at most.

17
00:00:52,652 --> 00:00:54,277
But a jet can chase the shadow.

18
00:00:54,428 --> 00:00:57,563
Flying with it at about four
hundred and sixty miles per hour,

19
00:00:57,644 --> 00:01:00,663
NASA planned to stretch that view
to nearly three minutes.

20
00:01:00,756 --> 00:01:02,474
Altitude mattered just as much.

21
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:07,049
At fifty thousand feet, the jet flies
above most clouds, dust and water vapor.

22
00:01:07,374 --> 00:01:10,044
Up there, the cameras
can also catch infrared light

23
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:13,585
that the lower atmosphere absorbs
before it ever reaches the ground.

24
00:01:14,282 --> 00:01:17,161
Before takeoff, the team
calculated exactly where the plane

25
00:01:17,196 --> 00:01:19,785
had to be as the shadow
moved across the ocean.

26
00:01:20,737 --> 00:01:24,696
Then the WB-57 took off
from a wet runway at Keflavik.

27
00:01:25,509 --> 00:01:28,527
Above the clouds,
the late-afternoon sky turned dark.

28
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,510
Pilot John Gustine held
the jet along the eclipse path,

29
00:01:32,951 --> 00:01:35,249
to stay in the shadow as long as possible.

30
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:38,071
In the back seat,
Cary Klemm ran the cameras,

31
00:01:38,117 --> 00:01:40,892
adjusting focus and exposure
through totality.

32
00:01:41,275 --> 00:01:43,051
Here is what the nose camera saw.

33
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:47,997
The corona, and pink prominences,
plumes of charged gas rising off the Sun.

34
00:01:48,438 --> 00:01:51,387
Below, students launched
balloons to measure how

35
00:01:51,457 --> 00:01:53,442
the sudden darkness changes the air.

36
00:01:53,488 --> 00:01:56,147
In Iceland, two teams
planned eighty launches,

37
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:59,630
from eighteen hours before the eclipse
to eight hours after.

38
00:01:59,688 --> 00:02:02,555
From the ground in Iceland,
clouds hid the eclipse.

39
00:02:02,706 --> 00:02:04,355
From the jet, the view was clear.

40
00:02:04,471 --> 00:02:07,780
The science team said it could not
have succeeded without the plane.

41
00:02:07,954 --> 00:02:09,486
Now they are studying the images,

42
00:02:09,545 --> 00:02:12,865
to learn how the corona is heated
and how it feeds the solar wind.

43
00:02:13,039 --> 00:02:16,766
For another place where Earth looks
different from above, watch this one next.
